    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is duct cleaning worth paying for, or is it mostly marketing?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Evidence decides it, not a schedule. Visible debris in the trunks, heavy lint at registers, dust blowing from vents at startup, or a filter loading in weeks all point to cleaning. Cleaning removes the debris an inspection found, and how much difference a household notices depends on how much was there and on filtration and return sealing. Where duct interiors are clean, or the real fault is leakage, filtration and sealing are the better spend."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What happens on a duct maintenance visit, and how long does it take?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A maintenance visit starts at the filter and blower compartment, then follows the accessible supply and return runs. Registers and grilles come off, airflow is measured at the furnace, and loose joints, crushed flex, closed dampers and blocked returns get noted. Most single-family inspections run one to two hours. City & Suburban inspects the accessible duct runs and quotes any cleaning scope after that inspection, with upfront pricing and no hidden fees."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How often do ducts need inspecting, and should it be booked with a furnace or AC tune-up?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A seasonal tune-up already reaches the filter, blower and nearest returns. Pairing a duct inspection with a heating tune-up in fall or an AC tune-up in spring keeps it to one trip. A closer survey of the runs makes sense every three to five years. That survey comes sooner after a remodel, after signs of rodents, or in a building with no service records. City & Suburban maintenance plans include seasonal check-ups, priority dispatch and member discounts."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What decides the price of duct maintenance work?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Scope and access decide most of it. The count of supply and return runs matters, and so does the share hidden behind finished ceilings. Tight basement or crawl space connections and the filter type add time. Inspection, register and blower cleaning, and full mechanical cleaning are three different jobs. City & Suburban puts the price in writing before work starts, with no hidden fees. The service-call offer in the pricing section excludes duct cleaning."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What should a duct cleaning quote in Chicago include before it is accepted?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A written scope settles most of the risk. It should list which runs are covered and the count of supply and return registers. It should also state whether access work is included, and what the inspection found before cleaning was added. Chemical fogging and whole-house coupon pricing quoted without an inspection are both warning signs. City & Suburban puts the price in writing before work starts, with no hidden fees."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why does one room stay cold while the next one overheats?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Air distribution usually explains it, not the heating equipment. A long branch to a back bedroom starves one room, and so does a crushed flex run, a damper left closed, or a sofa parked over a return. Balancing comes before any talk of new equipment: airflow measured room by room, dampers reset, returns cleared, registers adjusted. Where the imbalance survives balancing, leakage or an undersized run is the usual cause, which is duct repair work."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which filter suits the system, and how often does it need changing?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Fit and rating both decide this. A filter too restrictive for the blower cuts airflow, which can freeze a coil in July and overheat a furnace in January. A filter that rattles loose in its track lets dust bypass it entirely. Filter rating has to match the airflow the system actually has, and the filter has to seal in its track. One-inch filters generally need replacing every one to three months, and four-inch media filters often run six to twelve."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What signals that cleaning is not enough and the ducts need repair?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Symptoms that survive a fresh filter and open registers point at the duct itself. The common signals are whistling or booming at startup and a dusty smell that returns within days of a cleaning. Others are rooms that never reach temperature, visible gaps or hanging tape at joints, and a crawl space that feels oddly conditioned. An inspection separates those from debris, because sealing, reconnecting or resizing a run is duct repair rather than maintenance."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can duct maintenance cut down dust and allergy symptoms in the home?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The mechanism decides. Dust that reappears within days usually arrives through leaky returns, which pull air from a basement, crawl space or wall cavity, not off the duct walls. Return integrity and filter sealing come first: a sealed return and the right filter beat cleaning alone. Mold odor, vermin evidence or post-renovation debris makes cleaning the correct call. With no clear source, an indoor air quality assessment looks at humidity and ventilation."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is duct cleaning safe for the system, and what goes wrong with the wrong method?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Done properly it is routine work. Done with a shop vacuum and a stiff brush, it tears flex duct liner, pulls joints apart and drives debris deeper into the branches. Fogging chemicals into supply runs leaves coatings in the air a household breathes. Proper work contains the debris, protects the blower and coil, and seals leaks instead of spraying. Fiberglass duct board and brittle old flex need light handling or replacement."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What should happen when old wrap or tape turns up on a duct trunk in a 1920s house?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Gravity-furnace conversions often show oversized trunks feeding undersized branches, with wrap or tape of unknown material at the joints. The rule there is to stop: leave the suspect wrap undisturbed and have the material tested before any cleaning, brushing or sealing touches it. Aggressive cleaning is the wrong call on a duct run like that, because agitation puts whatever the wrap is made of into the air the household breathes."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "When should a duct problem be treated as urgent instead of routine?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Four situations should not wait. A sewer or exhaust odor from the vents is one, and soot marks around registers another. So are a carbon monoxide alarm that sounds and a return duct left open near a gas appliance. Each can pull combustion air into living space. Illinois regulates home alarms through the Carbon Monoxide Alarm Detector Act, and an alarm means fresh air and a phone call. City & Suburban offers same-day service when the schedule allows."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does duct work in Chicago need a permit, or is maintenance exempt?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Inspection, cleaning, filter changes and balancing are not permit work. A City of Chicago permit attaches to equipment and alteration jobs instead, such as replacing a furnace or altering a duct system as part of an installation. Chicago permit records for the 24 months to August 2026 include 81 single-scope furnace replacement permits. Where maintenance turns into reconnecting or resizing runs, the written quote should say whether a permit applies."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do rented units and condos need duct maintenance handled differently?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The heat obligation changes the urgency. Where a landlord supplies the heat, the Chicago Heat Ordinance sets a general minimum of 68 degrees in the daytime (Municipal Code 13-196-410). That standard runs from September 15 to June 1, with 66 degrees overnight. A closed-off return can put a unit under that line while the furnace runs normally. Findings like that belong in writing, so an owner or manager has something to act on."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which Chicago homes get the most out of duct maintenance?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Four groups benefit most. Homes with duct runs through an unconditioned basement, attic or crawl space come first. Next are homes remodeled since the last tune-up, then homes with pets and forced-air cooling. Last are new owners of a house with no service history. Cook County Assessor records for the eight Chicago townships put the median build year near 1927 for single-family and small multi-family homes, so many systems here are later retrofits."
     }
